// Phase 3C: `origin` through the physics seam — the two halves, side by side.
//
// Nothing else in this repository exercises a non-default `origin` on an
// object that has a body, so before this scene existed the whole
// origin-x-physics seam was verifiable only by arithmetic the headless suite
// checks against itself. Both halves are wrong in a way that still compiles,
// still type-checks, and shows up only in the geometry.
//
// LEFT — placement. `position` places the PILLAR's origin, which is its bottom
// edge, but Matter simulates a body about its centre of mass. The renderer
// reconciles the two: the body is created at the bounding-box centre and what
// the world reports is carried back to the origin point before painting.
// Look for: the pillar lands STANDING ON the ledge. If the reconciliation were
// dropped, the body would sit 105px (half its height) below the drawing, so
// the pillar would come to rest buried in the ledge or hanging above it.
//
// RIGHT — scale tracking. PixiJS scales an object about its pivot; Matter
// scales a body about the body's own centre. As the bottom-anchored BAR grows
// upward, its body's centre has to be moved up to match, or the collider stays
// where it started while the drawing climbs away from it.
// Look for: the RIDER ball is carried up by the growing bar and comes to rest
// ON its top edge. Untracked, the collider would grow downward through the
// ledge as much as upward, and the ball would stop roughly 80px short — with
// the bar drawn straight through it.
//
// The ledge freezes early (a numeric `duration` that expires), so it is
// immovable scenery by the time anything touches it. The pillar, the bar and
// the rider all declare `physics { duration: indefinitely }`, so their physics
// runners never complete and `isIdle()` deliberately does not wait on them.
// What makes this scene settle is the BODIES COMING TO REST: `isIdle()` also
// gates on `world.isIdle()`, which reports idle once every body is asleep or
// pinned. So `frozen at rest` and `deterministic` apply here because the
// simulation stops, not because the one animation finishes — a scene whose
// bodies never sleep has neither measure, however short its animations are.

let ink   = #e2e8f0
let slate = #334155
let sky   = #38bdf8
let amber = #fbbf24

let deck = 532

scene {
  size: (800, 600)
  background: #0a0e1a
  fit: contain

  text title {
    position: (400, 40)
    content: "origin x physics"
    fontSize: 20
    color: ink
  }

  rectangle ledge {
    position: (400, 545)
    size: (680, 26)
    color: slate
    physics {
      gravity: (0, 0)
      collideBounds: true
      duration: 0.4
    }
  }

  rectangle pillar {
    position: (250, 300)
    size: (80, 210)
    color: sky
    origin: (0.5, 1)
    physics {
      gravity: (0, 900)
      bounce: 0.05
      airDrag: 0.004
      collideBounds: true
      duration: indefinitely
    }
  }

  rectangle bar {
    position: (560, deck)
    size: (80, 120)
    color: amber
    origin: (0.5, 1)
    animate {
      property: scale
      to: (1, 2.4)
      duration: 1.4
      easing: easeOut
    }
    physics {
      gravity: (0, 0)
      bounce: 0
      collideBounds: true
      duration: indefinitely
    }
  }

  circle rider {
    position: (560, 390)
    radius: 20
    color: ink
    physics {
      gravity: (0, 900)
      bounce: 0.05
      collideBounds: true
      duration: indefinitely
    }
  }
}
